1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,543
Welcome to this explainer
for the CPA-TCP M4 module.

2
00:00:04,583 --> 00:00:04,893
Today,

3
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:09,835
we're going to build your pattern
recognition for personal financial planning.

4
00:00:09,875 --> 00:00:12,158
We're keeping it strictly to simple English,

5
00:00:12,198 --> 00:00:16,335
but we'll use the exact official tax
terminology you'll see on the exam.

6
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:17,736
Our ultimate goal here?

7
00:00:17,776 --> 00:00:25,918
We want you to deeply understand these rules so you can confidently
tackle multiple choice questions and task-based simulations.

8
00:00:25,958 --> 00:00:30,292
We are focusing heavily on application,
analysis, and those tricky calculations. So,

9
00:00:30,332 --> 00:00:31,773
let's dive right in.

10
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:37,127
Here's our quick roadmap for today
to keep everything super structured.

11
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:43,710
We're covering: 1. Retirement plans and
annuities. 2. Risk and investment return.

12
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:49,710
3. Education funding options. 4.
Insurance and risk mitigation.

13
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:53,107
5. Estate planning asset
transfers. And finally, 6.

14
00:00:53,147 --> 00:00:55,811
A rapid review for the final exam.

15
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:01,585
Alright, let's jump right into Section
1, Retirement Plans and Annuities.

16
00:01:01,625 --> 00:01:06,710
Let's start with a classic comparison,
traditional versus Roth accounts.

17
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:12,025
If you see this fact on the exam, pre-tax
contributions and taxable distributions,

18
00:01:12,065 --> 00:01:13,543
think traditional.

19
00:01:13,583 --> 00:01:18,752
You get a nice deduction now, but you're
going to pay ordinary income tax later.

20
00:01:18,792 --> 00:01:23,853
On the flip side, if you see after-tax
contributions and tax-free distributions,

21
00:01:23,893 --> 00:01:24,918
think Roth.

22
00:01:24,958 --> 00:01:29,668
There's no deduction up front, but
you get that sweet tax-free growth.

23
00:01:29,708 --> 00:01:33,335
So what fact matters most here?
It's the marginal tax rate.

24
00:01:33,375 --> 00:01:39,168
If current rates are higher than future
rates, traditional is your best bet.

25
00:01:39,208 --> 00:01:44,293
But if future rates are higher,
Roth is definitely the way to go.

26
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:47,009
Now, here's a huge exam trigger.

27
00:01:47,049 --> 00:01:52,127
A taxpayer with an employer
plan wants an IRA deduction.

28
00:01:52,167 --> 00:01:54,604
You absolutely must calculate the phase-out.

29
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:59,543
Don't just guess. Walk through
these exact logical steps.

30
00:01:59,583 --> 00:02:05,168
Let's say we have a single
taxpayer with an AGI of $81,000.

31
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:08,026
Step one, identify the threshold.

32
00:02:08,066 --> 00:02:14,418
Let's assume the phase-out begins
at $79,000 and has a $10,000 range.

33
00:02:14,458 --> 00:02:23,377
Step two, calculate the excess. $81,000
minus $79,000 gives us a $2,000 excess.

34
00:02:23,417 --> 00:02:32,585
Step three, get the percentage. That $2,000
divided by the $10,000 range is 20%.

35
00:02:32,625 --> 00:02:33,917
Finally, step four,

36
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:40,299
multiply that 20% by the maximum allowed
deduction and subtract that number from the limit.

37
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:43,627
Boom, that is your final deduction amount.

38
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:47,313
Looking at the broader
landscape of retirement plans,

39
00:02:47,353 --> 00:02:54,963
the main thing to remember is the difference in early
withdrawal penalties and required minimum distributions,

40
00:02:55,003 --> 00:02:55,877
or RMDs.

41
00:02:55,917 --> 00:02:59,708
If you see a distribution
before age 59 and a half,

42
00:02:59,748 --> 00:03:02,752
instantly think of the 10% penalty.

43
00:03:02,792 --> 00:03:08,460
But always, always check for exceptions,
like qualified medical expenses.

44
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:14,290
And remember this golden rule. RMDs
apply to traditional IRAs and 401(k)s,

45
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:17,418
but they do not apply to Roth IRAs.

46
00:03:17,458 --> 00:03:18,828
Moving on to annuities.

47
00:03:18,868 --> 00:03:22,997
If the exam asks you to calculate
the taxable portion of an annuity,

48
00:03:23,037 --> 00:03:24,377
how do you do it?

49
00:03:24,417 --> 00:03:31,907
You've got to use the exclusion ratio.
Think of an annuity like it has two parts,

50
00:03:31,947 --> 00:03:39,168
a non-taxable return of your original
investment and the taxable earnings.

51
00:03:39,208 --> 00:03:44,627
Step one, divide your original
investment by the total expected return.

52
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:50,252
That gives you the exclusion ratio. Step
two, multiply your payment by this ratio.

53
00:03:50,292 --> 00:03:54,553
This chunk is your tax-free
portion. Whatever's left over,

54
00:03:54,593 --> 00:03:58,127
that's your taxable income. Simple as that.

55
00:03:58,167 --> 00:04:03,085
Okay, let's switch gears and move to
Section 2, risk and investment return.

56
00:04:03,125 --> 00:04:06,543
Which rule applies when evaluating risk?

57
00:04:06,583 --> 00:04:11,168
Well, systematic risk
affects the whole economy.

58
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:18,918
Think about it like a rising tide.
You simply cannot diversify it away.

59
00:04:18,958 --> 00:04:22,741
Non-systematic risk, however,
is company-specific,

60
00:04:22,781 --> 00:04:27,585
meaning you actually can reduce
it through diversification.

61
00:04:27,625 --> 00:04:32,704
So for an exam trigger, if you see inflation
or global events, that's systematic.

62
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,663
But if a CEO unexpectedly resigns,
that's totally non-systematic.

63
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:40,059
Here's a crucial trap to avoid.

64
00:04:40,099 --> 00:04:44,668
A higher pre-tax return is
not always the better choice.

65
00:04:44,708 --> 00:04:48,752
You absolutely must calculate
the after-tax rate of return.

66
00:04:48,792 --> 00:04:54,960
Step one, calculate your initial ROI, which
is just your gain divided by your cost.

67
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,793
Step two, figure out the
total applicable tax rate.

68
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:03,543
Make sure to add up federal,
state, and net investment taxes.

69
00:05:03,583 --> 00:05:06,900
Step three, multiply your ROI
by one minus the tax rate.

70
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:10,816
This reveals what you actually
get to keep in your pocket.

71
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:11,423
Now,

72
00:05:11,463 --> 00:05:16,553
what fact matters most when you're choosing
between corporate bonds and municipal bonds?

73
00:05:16,708 --> 00:05:22,085
Think about it for a second. It all comes
down to the taxpayer's income tax bracket.

74
00:05:22,125 --> 00:05:23,719
A high-income taxpayer.

75
00:05:23,759 --> 00:05:28,620
Municipal bond interest is completely
exempt from federal income tax,

76
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:32,460
whereas corporate bond
interest is fully taxable.

77
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,794
So for a really high earner,

78
00:05:34,834 --> 00:05:43,835
a 4% municipal bond might actually give a much higher
after-tax rate of return than a 6% corporate bond.

79
00:05:43,875 --> 00:05:47,162
You have to always compare
those after-tax yields.

80
00:05:47,417 --> 00:05:51,918
All right, let's roll into Section
3, Education Funding Options.

81
00:05:51,958 --> 00:05:55,918
You really need to pay attention to the
distinct limits and conditions here.

82
00:05:55,958 --> 00:06:00,161
For Section 529 plans, there
is no strict age limit,

83
00:06:00,201 --> 00:06:07,095
and unused funds actually have a potential
to roll over to a Roth IRA after 15 years,

84
00:06:07,135 --> 00:06:09,752
which is an awesome feature.

85
00:06:09,792 --> 00:06:15,408
Coverdell accounts, though, much stricter.
The beneficiary must be under 18,

86
00:06:15,448 --> 00:06:19,918
and contributions are hard-capped
at just $2,000 a year.

87
00:06:19,958 --> 00:06:22,676
Then you have U.S. Series EE bonds,

88
00:06:22,716 --> 00:06:28,918
which are tax-exempt only if they're
used for qualified education expenses.

89
00:06:28,958 --> 00:06:30,894
Finally, UGMA or UTMA accounts.

90
00:06:30,934 --> 00:06:38,002
The big thing there is that control of the asset
transfers completely to the child at the age of majority.

91
00:06:38,042 --> 00:06:43,377
Let's look at a really common exam trap
regarding federal student financial aid.

92
00:06:43,417 --> 00:06:49,335
On the FAFSA, are 529 plans considered
a student asset or a parent asset?

93
00:06:49,375 --> 00:06:56,335
The main thing to remember is that student assets
heavily penalize financial aid eligibility.

94
00:06:56,375 --> 00:07:01,482
If a parent owns the 529
for a dependent student,

95
00:07:01,522 --> 00:07:08,168
FAFSA only expects up to 5.64%
of it to be used for college.

96
00:07:08,208 --> 00:07:15,252
But if an independent student owns it,
FAFSA expects up to 20% to be used.

97
00:07:15,292 --> 00:07:20,835
Who owns the account changes the
financial aid package entirely.

98
00:07:20,875 --> 00:07:26,085
Moving right along, here is Section
4, Insurance and Risk Mitigation.

99
00:07:26,125 --> 00:07:28,215
Whenever you see risk questions,

100
00:07:28,255 --> 00:07:32,585
instantly use this frequency
and severity grid in your head.

101
00:07:32,625 --> 00:07:37,773
If you see a fact pattern with high severity
but low frequency, like a house fire,

102
00:07:37,813 --> 00:07:39,377
think risk transfer.

103
00:07:39,417 --> 00:07:43,960
You buy insurance. High
frequency, low severity?

104
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,627
Think minor car scratches. That's
risk reduction or risk retention.

105
00:07:51,667 --> 00:07:56,347
And if something is both high frequency
and high severity, that's risk avoidance.

106
00:07:56,387 --> 00:07:59,460
You simply do not
participate in that activity.

107
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:05,377
You also need to clearly distinguish
term versus permanent life insurance.

108
00:08:05,417 --> 00:08:11,002
Term insurance covers a definite period
and has literally zero cash value.

109
00:08:11,042 --> 00:08:14,635
It's kind of like renting an apartment.
Permanent insurance, however,

110
00:08:14,675 --> 00:08:16,627
builds tax-deferred cash value.

111
00:08:16,667 --> 00:08:18,669
It's more like owning a home.

112
00:08:18,709 --> 00:08:24,752
Part of your premium actually builds equity
that you can borrow against later on.

113
00:08:24,792 --> 00:08:29,627
Let's dive into Section 5,
estate planning asset transfers.

114
00:08:29,667 --> 00:08:33,502
Beneficiary designations always beat a will.

115
00:08:33,542 --> 00:08:38,543
Assets labeled payable on death,
or POD, bypass probate entirely.

116
00:08:38,583 --> 00:08:44,881
So if a will says the son gets the money,
but the POD designation says the daughter,

117
00:08:44,921 --> 00:08:47,918
the daughter gets it. End of story.

118
00:08:47,958 --> 00:08:50,932
But watch out for this massive exam trap:

119
00:08:50,972 --> 00:08:54,835
bypassing probate does
not mean bypassing taxes.

120
00:08:54,875 --> 00:08:57,263
Probate avoidance is not tax avoidance.

121
00:08:57,303 --> 00:09:00,793
The asset still remains
firmly in the gross estate.

122
00:09:00,833 --> 00:09:05,635
And finally, let's wrap things up with
Section 6, Final Exam Rapid Review.

123
00:09:06,833 --> 00:09:10,011
Let's test your pattern recognition.

124
00:09:10,051 --> 00:09:20,752
A 45-year-old single taxpayer participates in an employer
401(k) and wants to deduct a traditional IRA contribution.

125
00:09:20,792 --> 00:09:25,252
Which rule applies here?
Identify your exam triggers.

126
00:09:25,292 --> 00:09:29,210
Fact one, they participate
in an employer plan.

127
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:33,960
Fact two, they want a
traditional IRA deduction.

128
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,960
The classic exam trap here is assuming that
all traditional IRAs are fully deductible.

129
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,680
They aren't. Because this
taxpayer has an employer plan,

130
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:51,252
you absolutely must test
the AGI phase-out limits.

131
00:09:51,292 --> 00:09:56,028
If they had no employer plan whatsoever,
they could take the full deduction,

132
00:09:56,068 --> 00:09:59,168
completely regardless of their income level.

133
00:09:59,208 --> 00:10:02,710
Finally, the number 59 and a half.

134
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:04,992
Burn this exact age into your memory.

135
00:10:05,032 --> 00:10:10,668
It is the primary threshold for avoiding
those nasty premature distribution penalties.

136
00:10:10,708 --> 00:10:13,918
As you prepare, don't just
memorize the dollar limits.

137
00:10:13,958 --> 00:10:15,138
Master the logic.

138
00:10:15,178 --> 00:10:20,710
Always ask yourself why one alternative
gives a better after-tax result.

139
00:10:20,750 --> 00:10:26,418
Are you ready to apply these exact
rules on exam day to beat the CPA traps?

140
00:10:26,458 --> 00:10:31,324
I know you are. Good luck with your
practice questions, and keep at it.
